From d423bfe9a89449608deaab967ceeea83712dbe8d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aileen Morgan <amorgan101010@protonmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:44:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] RingQueue for the HDI08 receive and interrupt queues: no heap
 use when warm

std::deque allocates and frees a block whenever its front or back
crosses a block boundary, even at a constant depth. The HDI08 receive
queue (fed per DSP word) and the pending-interrupt queues sit on the
audio thread, so a Machinedrum or Monomachine allocated ~270 times a
second while playing.

RingQueue is a power-of-two ring whose storage only grows, by doubling,
when the queue is deeper than it has ever been; within its high-water
mark it never touches the heap. It replaces std::deque for
Hdi08::m_rxData, Hdi08::m_pendingInterruptRequests and
Mc68k::m_pendingInterrupts (indexed reads and erase replace the
iterator loops). The transmit queue keeps std::deque: pollTx swaps it
with the caller's.

mc68kRingQueueTest checks it against std::deque over 200000 random
push, pop, erase and clear steps from capacity 4, so growth from a
wrapped state is covered.
